const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is air freight from China to Saudi Arabia?',
    a: 'Air freight is airport-to-airport transport from a Chinese gateway to Jeddah, Riyadh or Dammam, billed on chargeable weight (the higher of actual or volumetric ÷6000) and used where a 3–7 day window beats sea’s 18–45 days. It is chosen when speed, shelf life or value density justify the higher cost.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does air freight take from China to Saudi Arabia?',
    a: 'Indicative windows are 6–10 days for Shanghai–Riyadh (Freightos) and 2–5 business days in the cargofromchina market guide, with a practical planning shorthand of 3–7 days airport-to-airport. Door-to-door DDP air is commonly reported around 10–15 days. All are LOW-confidence research findings, not schedule guarantees — verify with the airline and forwarder.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does air freight cost from China to Saudi Arabia?',
    a: 'Indicative August 2026 market snapshots put general air freight around $880–$1,350 per 100 kg (roughly $8.80–$13.50 per kg), Freightos Shanghai–Riyadh at $917–$1,223 per 100 kg, and cargofromchina at SAR 26.5–30.5 per kg in higher weight brackets. These are LOW-confidence marketplace figures on different bases, not carrier quotes — verify before booking.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is air freight charged — actual weight or volumetric weight?',
    a: 'Air freight bills on the higher of actual and volumetric weight, where volumetric is L × W × H ÷ 6,000 — so a bulky 120,000 cm³ carton bills at 20 kg even if it weighs 12 kg. A dense carton is billed at actual weight instead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I calculate volumetric weight for air freight?',
    a: 'Measure the packed carton in centimetres, multiply length × width × height, then divide by 6,000. For example, a 60 × 50 × 40 cm carton is 120,000 cm³ ÷ 6,000 = 20 kg volumetric. If its actual weight is 12 kg, you pay for 20 kg. Confirm the carrier’s divisor because some services use 5,000.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Air freight or sea freight — which should I choose?',
    a: 'Run air’s 3–7 day window against sea’s 18–45 day window on three variables — time sensitivity, cargo value/density and total landed cost. Air is justified for urgent, high-value, perishable, fragile or small goods, while sea is usually cheaper for large, heavy, stable-demand cargo. SABER compliance applies to both, so air saves time, not paperwork.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What cargo should go by air freight?',
    a: 'The strongest air candidates are high-value-per-kilogram goods, perishables with short shelf life, fragile items where handling risk matters, small shipments where sea minimums are wasteful, and stockout-critical replenishment. Bulk commodities, dense machinery and stable-demand consumer goods usually belong on sea freight.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do SABER and FASAH apply to air freight?',
    a: 'Yes. Regulated goods still need a SABER Product Conformity Certificate (PC) and a shipment-specific SC before cargo arrival, and the pre-arrival declaration is still filed through FASAH. Air shortens transit but does not remove Saudi conformity or customs steps, so complete PC/SC work before the cargo lands.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which airports are used for China to Saudi Arabia air freight?',
    a: 'Eight named gateways anchor the corridor — PVG, HKG, CAN and SZX in China, and JED, RUH and DMM in Saudi Arabia — but airline and routing specifics were not published in the research snapshot. Confirm the airport pair and transit point with the carrier.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents do I need for air freight to Saudi Arabia?',
    a: 'Five core documents — the air waybill (AWB), commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin and the SABER conformity certificate for regulated goods — form the baseline set. Classify the goods with the correct HS code before quoting, because duty and conformity requirements depend on it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What hidden fees should I expect on air freight?',
    a: 'Beyond the per-kg rate, expect fuel and security surcharges, origin and destination terminal/handling charges, customs clearance and brokerage, SABER PC/SC fees, airport storage after free time, import duty of 5% CIF and VAT of 15% on CIF plus duty. Specific fee amounts were not published in the verified snapshot — request an itemised schedule.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is express courier the same as air freight?',
    a: 'No — express courier’s DHL benchmark of $76.36/kg at 1 kg (3–6 working days) is 5.7–8.7× air freight’s $8.80–$13.50/kg general bracket, buying door-to-door service and simpler billing. Air freight is usually airport-to-airport, billed on chargeable weight in larger consignments, and requires separate Saudi clearance.',
  },
];

const organization = {
  '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization`,
  name: 'WorldFreightHub',
  url: SITE_URL,
  logo: {
    '@type': 'ImageObject',
    url: `${SITE_URL}/favicon.svg`,
  },
};

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'Article',
    data: {
      headline: 'Air Freight from China to Saudi Arabia: Rates, Transit & Chargeable Weight (August 2026)',
      description:
        'Air freight from China to Saudi Arabia explained: chargeable vs volumetric weight, indicative $/kg rate benchmarks, 3–7 day transit planning, air vs sea decision framework, airports and cost breakdown.',
      datePublished: '2026-08-31',
      dateModified: '2026-08-31',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      mainEntityOfPage: {
        '@type': 'WebPage',
        '@id': pageUrl,
      },
      author: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'WorldFreightHub',
        url: SITE_URL,
      },
      publisher: {
        '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization`,
      },
      image: `${SITE_URL}/og.png`,
    },
  },
  {
    type: 'FAQPage',
    data: {
      mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: faq.q,
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: faq.a,
        },
      })),
    },
  },
  {
    type: 'Organization',
    data: organization,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Air Freight from China to Saudi Arabia: Rates, Transit & Chargeable Weight (August 2026)"
  description="Air freight from China to Saudi Arabia: chargeable vs volumetric weight, indicative $/kg rate benchmarks, 3–7 day transit planning, air vs sea decision framework, airports and cost breakdown."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Middle East', href: '/middleeast/' },
          { label: 'Air freight from China to Saudi Arabia' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">August 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Air freight from China to Saudi Arabia: rates, transit &amp; chargeable weight</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A decision-first guide to flying goods from China to Jeddah, Riyadh and Dammam. This page
        explains how airlines actually bill you — chargeable weight, not just actual weight — then
        gives indicative rate and transit windows, the air-versus-sea trade-off, and the airports
        and cost stack that make up a realistic landed-cost budget.
      </p>

      <section class="section container">
        <blockquote class="tldr">
          <p>
            <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Air freight cuts China–Saudi planning transit from an 18–45 day sea window to 3–7 days airport-to-airport — a 15-day saving at the low end and 38 days at the high end. Chargeable weight, not actual weight, drives the bill: a 60 × 50 × 40 cm carton (120,000 cm³ ÷ 6000) bills at 20 kg versus a 12 kg actual weight, a 67% uplift. The ÷6000 versus ÷5000 divisor is a 20% breakpoint — the same 120,000 cm³ carton becomes 24 kg on a courier’s ÷5000 formula, so the mode decision starts with chargeable-weight math.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative market snapshots. Treat every
        LOW-confidence rate, transit and routing note as indicative, verify with carrier before
        relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Saudi import VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">15% <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Baseline import duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% CIF <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">SABER SC rule</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Before arrival <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Air planning transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">3–7 days <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Sea comparison transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">18–25 days <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Volumetric divisor</span>
          <span class="fact-value">L×W×H/6000 <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. The billing rule most shippers miss: chargeable weight</h2>
    <p>
      Air freight is not billed on actual weight alone. Airlines bill on <strong>chargeable
      weight</strong>, which is the higher of the actual gross weight and the volumetric weight of
      the packed shipment. Light, bulky goods are charged for the space they occupy, and that is the
      single most common reason a first-time air shipper is surprised by the final invoice.
    </p>
    <p>
      The standard IATA volumetric formula is <strong>L × W × H (cm) ÷ 6,000</strong>. Some express
      or dense-cargo services use a divisor of 5,000, which makes the volumetric result even larger,
      so always confirm the divisor with the airline or forwarder before comparing quotes.
    </p>

    <h3>Worked examples</h3>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'example', label: 'Shipment' },
        { key: 'actualWeight', label: 'Actual weight' },
        { key: 'dimensions', label: 'Dimensions' },
        { key: 'volumetricWeight', label: 'Volumetric weight' },
        { key: 'chargeableWeight', label: 'Chargeable weight' },
        { key: 'note', label: 'What it means' },
      ]}
      rows={volumetricRows}
      caption="Volumetric weight uses the IATA 6,000 divisor. Chargeable weight is the higher of actual and volumetric weight."
    />
    <SourceList sources={[iata]} title="Sources — chargeable weight" />

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>The comparison trap:</strong> never compare two air quotes on $/kg of actual weight.
      Compute the chargeable weight for each quote using the same divisor, then compare the total
      chargeable kilograms and the all-in line items.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Indicative air freight rates from China to Saudi Arabia</h2>
      <p>
        Air rates change weekly with fuel, capacity, season and airport pairing, so these market
        snapshots are deliberately labelled LOW confidence. The sources use different weight
        brackets, route and date bases, so do not average them together.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'service', label: 'Air service' },
          { key: 'benchmark', label: 'Published benchmark' },
          { key: 'perKg', label: 'Indicative per kg' },
          { key: 'transit', label: 'Indicative transit' },
          { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={airRateRows}
        caption="Indicative market snapshots, not carrier quotes. Per-kg figures are arithmetic conversions from published 100 kg brackets where relevant. Verify with a forwarder before relying on these figures."
      />
      <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — air freight rates" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Headline rate vs landed cost:</strong> the per-kg rate is only one line. Fuel and
        security surcharges, terminal handling, Saudi clearance, SABER and duty/VAT all sit on top,
        so request an all-in itemised quote for a realistic comparison.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Typical air transit windows from China to Saudi Arabia</h2>
    <p>
      Air transit depends on the airport pair, direct versus transhipped routing, and how quickly
      Saudi clearance finishes after landing. Use these windows for planning, then confirm the
      flight routing and the clearance lead time.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'lane', label: 'Lane' },
        { key: 'service', label: 'Service' },
        { key: 'transit', label: 'Indicative transit' },
        { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={airTransitRows}
      caption="Indicative transit windows only — verify with the relevant airline or forwarder before relying on these figures."
    />
    <SourceList sources={transitSources} title="Sources — air transit times" />

    <p class="small muted mt-2">
      The 3–7 day planning shorthand is a tighter airport-to-airport view for direct moves; the
      full research snapshot is broader at 2–10 days depending on service. Door-to-door DDP air is
      longer because it adds clearance and final delivery.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. Air vs sea: when the speed is worth the premium</h2>
      <p>
        Sea is usually cheaper per unit, but air wins when time sensitivity, shelf life or value
        density dominate. The correct test is not “air is always expensive” — it is whether the
        inventory, cash and risk costs of a slower sea move exceed the air premium.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'factor', label: 'Factor' },
          { key: 'air', label: 'Air freight' },
          { key: 'sea', label: 'Sea freight' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={airVsSeaRows}
        caption="Decision factors are heuristic; unit-cost and transit figures are LOW confidence. Verify with your forwarder for the actual shipment."
      />
      <SourceList sources={comparisonSources} title="Sources — air vs sea" />

      <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
        <Card title="Choose air freight when...">
          <ul>
            <li>The goods are urgent, perishable or stockout-critical.</li>
            <li>Value per kilogram is high and damage risk matters.</li>
            <li>The shipment is small and sea minimum charges are wasteful.</li>
            <li>A 3–7 day arrival cycle is worth the premium.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Choose sea freight when...">
          <ul>
            <li>Cargo is large, dense, heavy or stable-demand.</li>
            <li>Unit cost matters more than transit time.</li>
            <li>The shipment fills a meaningful share of a container.</li>
            <li>The consignee can absorb an 18–45 day window.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
